From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 18:06:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 96/97] fix(reap): read beads through a rig-pinned bd, keeping
 gc bd as the fallback
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The witness reaper starts and examines nothing. One `gc bd` call costs ~5.4s on
a rig with an external Dolt (0.5s here), and a cycle makes two bead reads inside
an 8s self-budget — so the budget is routinely gone before the first `git
status` runs. That is the 17 `budget_spent_before_git_status` lines in the reap
log, and it is why a rig with more candidates than seconds examines NOTHING
rather than examining fewer.

Both bead reads now go through one helper, `bead_read`, which prefers a
`-C`-pinned read-only `bd` rooted at $RIG_ROOT and keeps `gc bd` as the
fallback. The budget model, `run_bounded`, `classify_outcome` and every reason
code are untouched: this substitutes the transport, nothing else. Routing both
reads through one helper also stops the gate read and the point-of-use re-check
from ever landing on different ledgers.

Measured on gascity-packs, 12 candidates, dry-run:

    budget 8s   before: scan_complete, 4s left   after: scan_complete, 5s left
    budget 3s   before: budget_exhausted, 1 of 12   after: scan_complete, 12/12
    budget 2s   before: budget_exhausted, 1 of 12   after: unchanged

The 2s row is the honest bound. The roster read is still `gc session list`,
which has no pinned equivalent, so this buys back roughly two fixed-overhead
units — decisive on a winnow-class rig, not a guarantee that every cycle now
completes.

The pinned answer is trusted only when it resolves at least one of the exact
ids asked for. A store that knows none of them is likelier the wrong store than
a rig where every worktree is a non-bead, and guessing wrong there is not a slow
cycle: an all-absent batch pushes every candidate onto the no-such-bead route,
which decides on the worktree's own evidence.

On the bare-bd guard: `tests/test_no_bare_bd_commands.py` is about routing, not
about the binary — it forbids a pack asset reading whichever store it is
standing in. `-C` pins the project to a named directory and fails closed on a
directory with no beads project rather than walking up to $PWD, so the guard was
amended narrowly rather than worked around. The new allowlist is triple-locked
like the one already there (exact path, literal marker, byte-exact line) and
holds exactly one entry; new tests assert that a copy elsewhere, an edit in
place, the marker on any other bd call, and a bare `--dir` form all still fail,
and that `gc bd` is still built and reachable.

Transport equivalence verified on every endpoint origin in this city —
inherited_city, managed_city, and explicit (winnow's external Dolt on
127.0.0.1:3307) — all returning identical {id,status,assignee}. That closes the
acceptance item the dispatch recorded as unsatisfiable: a suspended rig is not
an unreachable endpoint.

The reap-log failure lines said "bulk gc bd show" unconditionally. With two
possible transports that becomes a gcp-mqu9 repeat, so the detail now names the
transport that actually failed, and the mol-witness-patrol log-review table was
corrected with it: its "a timeout with most of the budget unspent is the rig's
Dolt endpoint" branch is the misdirection two witnesses already flagged on the
bead, and one wrapped call is most of an 8s budget on its own.

gcp-uzq0

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
